# Azure Active Directory Configuration Example
# 
# IMPORTANT: This is an example file. Copy this to a secure location and never commit
# the actual values to source control.
#
# For Azure App Service, configure these as Application Settings:
# az webapp config appsettings set --resource-group <rg> --name <app-name> --settings KEY=VALUE
#
# For local development, you can use environment variables or a .env file (add .env to .gitignore)

# Microsoft Graph API Configuration
# Required for On-Behalf-Of (OBO) flow to access Microsoft Graph
GRAPH_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id-here
GRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret-here
GRAPH_TENANT=your-tenant-id-or-domain-here

# Example values (DO NOT USE THESE IN PRODUCTION):
# GRAPH_CLIENT_ID=fee96351-9eda-4fb7-a91b-ac46e9c07358
# GRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET=your-secret-from-azure-ad
# GRAPH_TENANT=thx1139corp.onmicrosoft.com

# Configuration Instructions:
# 
# 1. Create an Azure AD App Registration or use an existing one
# 2. Generate a client secret in Azure AD
# 3. Configure API permissions for Microsoft Graph (openid, profile, email)
# 4. Set these values in Azure App Service Application Settings
# 5. For Azure Key Vault (recommended for production):
#    GRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET=@Microsoft.KeyVault(VaultName=your-vault;SecretName=GraphClientSecret)

# Additional Security Notes:
# - Rotate secrets regularly (every 90 days recommended)
# - Use different secrets for different environments (dev, staging, prod)
# - Monitor and audit secret access
# - Use Managed Identity when possible to avoid storing secrets
